  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Kansas received $2.6B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Award activity in the state shows 19,534 awards with an average award size of $133K. Obligations fell 27.4% year over year. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the single largest buyer in the state with $1.5B in obligations, followed by the Department of Defense with $474M and the Department of Transportation with $456M. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry by obligations with $1.3B. ORACLE HEALTH GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. leads vendors with $1.3B in obligations, and INDRA AIR TRAFFIC, INC. follows with $374M. Historical context shows federal obligations to Kansas have varied by fiscal year: $2.9B in FY21, $4.2B in FY22, $3B in FY23, $3.3B in FY24, $3.6B in FY25, and $2.6B in FY26. These figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How do FY2026 obligations compare with prior years?",
        "body": "Kansas recorded $2.6B in FY26 obligations, below FY25 obligations of $3.6B. Kansas obligations peaked at $4.2B in FY22 and were $2.9B in FY21, $3B in FY23, and $3.3B in FY24. Obligations fell 27.4% year over year from FY25 to FY26."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which buyers and industries dominate federal spending in Kansas?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $1.5B and is the top buyer in Kansas for FY26 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $474M and the Department of Transportation obligated $456M. Computer Systems Design Services accounted for $1.3B in industry obligations, and ORACLE HEALTH GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. accounted for $1.3B among vendors."
      }
    ]
  },
